Accessibility: holding a button instead of mashing it in the minigames (GOW_QTE_HOLD).
- gow_qte_hold.h (new): a button the player keeps down becomes the train of presses the game's minigames ask for
  ("Tap O" on a grapple or a context kill). A press shorter than the delay goes through untouched, so single presses
  keep their meaning; inside each press the button reads as released only long enough for one pad poll (the game polls
  once per frame), so it is down about two thirds of the time. No dependency on the runtime, so the logic is covered by
  tools/tests/qte_hold_test.cpp.
- ps2_pad.cpp: GOW_QTE_HOLD="circle,cross" (names of kNombresAccion; "1" = circle) chooses the buttons,
  GOW_QTE_HOLD_HZ the presses per second (10), GOW_QTE_HOLD_DELAY the hold in ms before it starts (250),
  GOW_QTE_HOLD_RELEASE the released part of each press in ms (34), GOW_QTE_HOLD_DIAG=1 logs each press. It is applied
  to the finished button word, so controller, configurable keyboard and the fixed layouts behave the same. Without the
  variable nothing changes.
The launcher's Controls tab writes the variables ("Hold instead of mashing"), for the face buttons only: L1, R1, L2 and
R2 are held in normal play and repeating them would drop a block.
In game, GOW_QTE_HOLD_DIAG=1 logs each press the assist makes and the runtime's own GOW_PAD_LOG=1 prints the packet the
game reads.
Not tried in game yet: the rate the minigames need, and whether the brief releases disturb a "Hold" prompt on the same
button. A later patch could narrow the assist to the minigames themselves (Kratos's move id, as gow_cancelar.cpp reads
it, or the minigame prompt the icon code recognises) instead of any held press.

